HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements, and the three buses along the bottom say it once for each family: three senders, four guards, one retry '
 'policy. Filled diamond = owns: the service owns one record per notification and one gate per recipient, and a record '
 'owns its progress. Plain arrow = references. Dashed green = handed in through <code>configure(...)</code>; the '
 'template engine, the directory and the two stores come in the same way, and are left unlabelled only to keep the '
 'picture readable. Dotted blue = notifies. <b>Where state lives:</b> a notification holds nothing that moves &mdash; '
 'every field is final &mdash; and neither does a recipient, a rendered message, a guard or a sender. Everything that '
 'changes is in two places: the <code>Progress</code> inside a record, swapped as one object by compare-and-set, and '
 'the service\'s own queue, ledger and gates. Notice what is <i>not</i> here: no Message class separate from '
 'Notification, because the words are rendered once at submit and stored on the record; no Retry class, because a retry '
 'is a state plus a scheduled re-queue; and no Boolean anywhere near the word "sent". Eight classes in '
 'Main.java are off the picture on purpose, because they add nothing to the shape: the in-memory TemplateEngine, '
 'RecipientDirectory, PreferenceStore and DedupeStore the tests hand in, the MessageTemplate one of them holds, '
 'the ConsoleAudit and Metrics listeners, and TimerScheduler &mdash; plus Main itself, which only runs the demo.')
print("uml built")

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy '
 'button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with '
 'an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (ten blocks of claims proven; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (idempotency and the delivery guarantee first); then type '
 'in the order of Main.java: the four enums with DeliveryState carrying a rank, SendResult and Verdict, the Notification '
 'with its builder, Clock and Scheduler, the template engine and the directory, the Sender interface with three '
 'implementations, the DeliveryGuard interface with four, the retry policy, the listeners, Progress and DeliveryRecord '
 'with moveTo, then NotificationService with submit, the worker loop and deliver, and finally a main with a fifty-thread '
 'race on one de-dup key.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Minute 35. Compliance says no notifications between 9pm and 8am local time, except OTPs.", "twist", 10,
 "This is the question the whole shape exists for, and the answer is a new file. Quiet hours has nothing in common "
 "with the rules already there &mdash; it needs the recipient's timezone, it only applies to channels that buzz a "
 "phone, and CRITICAL overrides it &mdash; so in an if-ladder version it would be a fourth clause bolted into "
 "<code>submit</code> and a fresh chance to break de-dup. Here it is one class implementing DeliveryGuard and one "
 "entry in the list handed to <code>configure</code>; the engine, the queue, the senders and the state machine are "
 "untouched. The one design decision worth saying out loud: it DEFERS rather than suppresses, because a receipt the "
 "user wanted at 3am is still wanted at 8am, and deferring reuses the same scheduler the retries already use. The "
 "second snippet is the guard next to it, and it shows the other half of the contract: a guard that RESERVES "
 "something also implements <code>undo</code>, so a message refused by a later guard does not eat the user's quota.",
 sect(src, "final class QuietHoursGuard", "final class DailyCapGuard") + "\n"
 + sect(src, "final class DailyCapGuard", "final class DedupeGuard")),
("Two threads submit the same idempotency key at the same instant. Prove the human gets one message, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between \"has this key been claimed?\" and \"claim it\". <code>putIfAbsent</code> is one "
 "operation: it returns the previous value, so exactly one of fifty threads sees null and wins, and the other "
 "forty-nine get a verdict with a reason on it. The claim is taken LAST, after every other guard has said yes, so a "
 "message we were going to refuse anyway does not burn the key. The proof is not a count of records &mdash; it is a "
 "count of gateway calls: fifty threads wait on one latch, all fifty submit the same key, and the test then asks the "
 "fake push provider how many times it was actually called. One. It also checks that exactly one key is held in the "
 "store, not fifty, which is what catches a leaked reservation.",
 T("        // 1. fifty threads", "        // 2. the gateway times out")),
("One gate per user. Does that scale, or have you serialised the notification service?", "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and move 8 has the arithmetic: 1.2 microseconds inside the gate, against a gate that any one user "
 "touches a handful of times a second. What this card adds is the shape of the code that makes that number true, "
 "and the one case where it is not. In the code: the message is rendered BEFORE the gate is taken, so the part "
 "that can throw cannot leave a quota half-spent; the gate is released BEFORE anything is enqueued; and nothing "
 "inside it can block. The case where it is not true is a single hot userId &mdash; an alerting bot hammering one "
 "service account &mdash; and the honest answer is that the gate then serialises that account at roughly eight "
 "hundred thousand admissions a second, which is still far more than the gateway behind it can take. So the thing "
 "that actually pushes back is the queue, and it does: when it is full, submit undoes every reservation and "
 "returns a record saying <code>back-pressure: queue full</code>, which is test 9.",
 sect(src, "DeliveryRecord submit(Notification n)", "DeliveryState statusOf(String")),
("The SMS gateway times out. Did the message go out or not? What do you write down?", "functional", 10,
 "You do not know, and the design says so: the outcome of a send is SENT, PERMANENT, TRANSIENT or UNKNOWN, and a "
 "timeout is UNKNOWN. The record moves to SENDING before the call and to SENT only after the gateway has answered, "
 "so a crash in between leaves it saying SENDING &mdash; which is the truth &mdash; instead of a boolean that is "
 "wrong either way. A timeout is then retried, with the SAME idempotency key, because the only place a duplicate "
 "can actually be removed is the provider. That is the honest position: exactly-once does not exist across a "
 "network you do not control, so you pick at-least-once plus an idempotent receiver, which is effectively-once. If "
 "the budget runs out while still UNKNOWN, the answer is not to guess: query the provider by the key and take "
 "their answer.",
 sect(src, "private void deliver(DeliveryRecord record)", "private void retryOrGiveUp") + "\n"
 + X("exactly once is impossible", "jittered backoff")),
("Support asks \"what happened to notification n-8, and why did my user get nothing\" a thousand times a second.", "non-functional", 5,
 "One hash lookup. The ledger is a map from id to record, and the record carries the whole answer as one immutable "
 "triple: which attempt, which state, and the reason in plain words &mdash; \"preference: opted out of marketing on "
 "PUSH\", \"daily-cap: already had 20 today\", \"dedupe: duplicate of login-77\". That is why a suppressed message "
 "still gets a record instead of being dropped silently: \"why did my user get nothing\" is the most common support "
 "question there is, and a system that answers it from logs is a system that answers it in twenty minutes. The read "
 "takes no lock at all &mdash; the progress lives in an AtomicReference, so a reader either sees the old triple or "
 "the new one and never a half-written mixture.",
 sect(src, "DeliveryState statusOf(String", "boolean onReceipt(String")),
("Providers now post delivery receipts, and they arrive late and out of order. SENT is not the end.", "twist", 5,
 "Nothing structural changes, because the rank rule was built for exactly this. Every state carries a number, every "
 "move is a compare-and-set that only lands if it raises the rank, and non-terminal ranks are scoped to the attempt "
 "&mdash; <code>attempt &times; 100 + the state's number</code> &mdash; so a receipt for attempt 1 arriving while "
 "attempt 2 is in flight is dropped without touching anything. DELIVERED is terminal and ranks above every other "
 "terminal state, including FAILED_FINAL, and that is deliberate: if the handset acknowledged it, it arrived, "
 "whatever we had already written down after giving up. The webhook itself is a listener that remembers which "
 "provider reference belongs to which record and attempt, so the HTTP handler is three lines.",
 sect(src, "record Progress(int attempt", "final class DeliveryRecord") + "\n"
 + X("delivery receipts", "priority lanes")),
("A flood of CRITICAL alerts means marketing never goes out at all. Fix the starvation.", "twist", 5,
 "Say the trade first, because a single priority queue really does have this failure: ordering strictly by class "
 "means a permanent flood of the top class starves the bottom one for ever. Two answers. The cheap one is ageing: "
 "a message's effective class improves the longer it has waited, so nothing waits indefinitely, at the cost of "
 "ordering that is no longer purely by class. The real one is a lane per class &mdash; its own workers, its own "
 "bound &mdash; so each class is guaranteed a share of the pool instead of competing for it. The engine does not "
 "change at all; only the routing in front of it does, which is the test that it was factored correctly.",
 X("priority lanes", "batching for email")),
("The SMS provider is throttling us and every worker is parked on a socket to a gateway that is down.", "twist", 8,
 "Both answers are the same shape: a class that implements Sender and holds a Sender, so the engine cannot tell "
 "the difference. The circuit breaker counts consecutive failures and, past a threshold, answers "
 "TRANSIENT_FAILURE without making a network call for the next minute &mdash; so a dead gateway costs nothing "
 "instead of parking the pool, and one success closes it again. The rate limiter holds a token bucket per user and "
 "channel, where the bucket is one immutable record (tokens plus the instant they were measured) inside an "
 "AtomicReference, so \"refill by elapsed time, then spend one\" is a single compare-and-set and two threads "
 "cannot both spend the last token. Being refused is a TRANSIENT failure, which means the engine's own backoff "
 "does the waiting, on the timer, not on a worker. There is a third failure hiding behind those two, and it is "
 "the one interviewers ask for by name: when the dead gateway comes back, ten thousand parked retries are all due "
 "in the same millisecond and knock it over again. <code>JitteredBackoff</code> wraps any retry policy and spreads "
 "each wait across the top half of its range, so three messages that would all have waited 2000 ms wait 1730, 1749 "
 "and 1348 instead. It is a Decorator as well, which is why it is three lines.",
 X("a flaky provider", "the provider caps us") + "\n" + X("the provider caps us", "delivery receipts")
 + "\n" + X("jittered backoff", "one message, several channels")),
("Twenty alerts in a minute should be one email. And a million-message campaign must not become one spike.", "twist", 5,
 "Both live above the engine, not inside it. Batching cannot be a Sender: a Sender that buffered would have to "
 "answer SENT before the provider had seen anything, and that lie is exactly what the three-way SendResult exists "
 "to prevent. So a collector holds lines for a window or until it is full, then submits ONE notification whose "
 "parameters are the joined lines &mdash; and every guard, retry and receipt then applies to the digest, unchanged. "
 "Pacing is the same idea in time: the campaign is chunked and each chunk is handed to the same injected Scheduler "
 "the retries use, so a million messages spread across an hour. Both are worth naming as policy rather than "
 "mechanism, because that is why neither one needed a change to the engine.",
 X("batching for email", "campaign pacing") + "\n" + X("campaign pacing", "persistence and the outbox")),
("Push failed, so send it by SMS. And if the user turned push off, what then?", "twist", 8,
 "A ladder of channels, and like batching it lives above the engine rather than inside it. "
 "<code>ChannelLadder</code> is a listener: it remembers which rung each record it started is on, and when one "
 "ends in FAILED_FINAL or EXPIRED it submits the same message on the next channel. Every rung is a real "
 "notification with its own record, its own guards and its own retries, which is what keeps &ldquo;where is "
 "n-8&rdquo; answerable &mdash; one record per send, never one record carrying three outcomes. The second half of "
 "the question is what separates a good answer from a careless one: only a FAILURE climbs. A SUPPRESSED does not, "
 "because a mute, a daily cap or a duplicate is a decision somebody made, and texting a user because they turned "
 "push off is a compliance incident, not a fallback. The listener runs after the transition has been committed and "
 "with no lock held, which is why calling submit from inside it is safe; in production the next rung goes to the "
 "same Scheduler the retries use, so no worker is spent on admission. Test 10 proves both halves: a missing device "
 "token climbs exactly one rung and the SMS gateway is called once; a muted push climbs none and it is called not "
 "at all.",
 X("one message, several channels", "ten instances behind") + "\n"
 + T("        PushSender pushB = new PushSender();", "        System.out.println(failures == 0")),
("Persist it. And now there are ten instances behind a load balancer.", "twist", 8,
 "Two tables and two shared stores, and the engine does not change, only what it was handed. The rank rule maps "
 "straight onto SQL: <code>moveTo</code> becomes <code>UPDATE ... SET state = ?, rank = ? WHERE id = ? AND rank "
 "&lt; ?</code>, and the row count is the boolean &mdash; the database refuses the stale write exactly as the "
 "compare-and-set does. The de-dup claim becomes <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code> or Redis "
 "<code>SET key NX PX ttl</code>, which is the same single atomic claim <code>putIfAbsent</code> was, which is why "
 "it needed no lock in the first place. The queue becomes a broker with a topic per priority class, and the "
 "hand-off from submit to queue becomes an outbox row written in the same transaction as the record, so a crash "
 "between \"accepted\" and \"queued\" is recovered rather than lost.",
 X("persistence and the outbox", "exactly once is impossible") + "\n" + X("ten instances behind", "Runs every extension")),
("Add WhatsApp. And Slack for internal alerts.", "functional", 5,
 "One class per provider implementing Sender, one line in the map handed to <code>configure</code>, and one "
 "template registered for that channel. No engine edits: the queue, the guards, the retry policy and the state "
 "machine do not know how many channels exist. The one cost that is not free is worth pointing at rather than "
 "hiding: Channel is an enum, so a real new channel needs a constant, and the exhaustive switch in "
 "<code>Recipient.addressFor</code> then refuses to compile until you say which address it uses &mdash; which is "
 "the compiler doing your code review. Key the registry by String instead and even that disappears, at the cost of "
 "typos becoming runtime errors.",
 X("a new channel", "a flaky provider")),
("Where does time come from, and how do you test quiet hours and backoff without sleeping?", "design", 3,
 "Two seams, both one-method interfaces, both handed in through configure. <code>Clock</code> answers \"what time "
 "is it\", so a test stands at 03:00 for a recipient at UTC+5 by handing in a lambda. <code>Scheduler</code> "
 "answers \"run this later\", so production hands in one shared timer thread &mdash; never a sleeping worker "
 "&mdash; and a test hands in one that runs the task immediately. That is why the whole failure suite finishes in "
 "well under a second with no sleeps anywhere: the retry loop runs at full speed while the backoff arithmetic is "
 "asserted separately, exactly and on its own.",
 "/** Where time comes from, and where \"later\" comes from. Both injected, both one method. */\n"
 "interface Clock     { long nowMs(); }\n"
 "interface Scheduler { void schedule(Runnable task, long delayMs); }\n\n"
 "// production: one shared timer thread, so ten thousand pending retries cost ten thousand small objects\n"
 "service.configure(templates, people, policy, guards, senders, System::currentTimeMillis, new TimerScheduler());\n\n"
 "// a test: stand at 03:00 local for a UTC+5 recipient, and run every retry inline\n"
 "long threeAm = atLocalHour(3, 5);\n"
 "Scheduler inline = (task, delayMs) -> task.run();\n"
 "service.configure(templates, people, policy, List.of(quiet), senders, () -> threeAm, inline);\n\n"
 "// and assert the arithmetic on its own, with no engine involved at all\n"
 "RetryPolicy backoff = new ExponentialBackoff(4, 100, 10_000);\n"
 "assert backoff.backoffMs(1) == 100 && backoff.backoffMs(2) == 200 && backoff.backoffMs(3) == 400;\n"
 "assert backoff.backoffMs(20) == 10_000;                 // capped, not overflowed to a year\n"),
("Which pattern is where, which SOLID letter is where, and where would a Factory or a Builder earn its place?", "design", 8,
 "Moves 10 and 11 already put every pattern and every letter next to the move that produced it, so this card is "
 "the exhibit instead: one line of real code each, to point at in the room rather than describe. What is worth "
 "arguing out loud is the three judgements at the bottom of it. Builder is earned here: a notification has seven "
 "fields of which four are optional, and a seven-argument constructor with three nulls in it is exactly how a "
 "promotional message goes out on somebody's OTP template. Singleton is not earned: the service is handed to its "
 "callers, which is why the failure suite builds ten of them and they never see each other. Factory is not earned "
 "yet: the channel-to-sender map already <i>is</i> the registry, and it gets the name the day channels arrive as "
 "strings from configuration, because that is the day a switch on a string appears and needs somewhere to live. Of "
 "the five letters, the one an interviewer actually tests is O, by changing a rule mid-round: quiet hours arrived "
 "after the design was written and cost one new file and one entry in a list.",
 "// Strategy: the one line that differs per provider, behind an interface, handed in and never built here\n"
 "interface Sender { Channel channel(); SendResult send(Recipient to, RenderedMessage m, String idempotencyKey); }\n\n"
 "// Chain of Responsibility: independent reasons, in order, and the first refusal stops the walk\n"
 "for (DeliveryGuard g : guards) { Verdict v = g.check(n, to, now); if (v.kind() != ALLOW) { verdict = v; break; } reserved.add(g); }\n"
 "if (verdict.kind() == SUPPRESS) for (int i = reserved.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) reserved.get(i).undo(n, to);\n\n"
 "// Decorator: a Sender that holds a Sender, so the engine cannot tell the difference\n"
 "Map.of(Channel.SMS, new CircuitBreakerSender(new RateLimitedSender(sms, 50, 10, clock), 2, 60_000, clock));\n\n"
 "// Observer: announced after the change is committed, one try/catch each\n"
 "for (DeliveryListener l : listeners) try { l.onTransition(this, from, to, detail); } catch (RuntimeException ignored) {}\n\n"
 "// State: one compare-and-set, and only if the rank goes UP\n"
 "if (want <= current.rank()) return false;\n\n"
 "// Builder: seven fields, four optional -- this is where a builder stops being ceremony\n"
 "Notification.to(\"u1\", Channel.SMS, \"otp\").priority(Priority.CRITICAL).dedupeKey(\"login-77\")\n"
 "            .category(\"otp\").params(Map.of(\"code\", \"4821\")).deadlineMs(now + 60_000).build();\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. The registry is already here; it earns the name when channels come from config strings\n"
 "Map<String, Sender> byName = Map.of(\"EMAIL\", new EmailSender(), \"WHATSAPP\", new WhatsAppSender());\n"),
]

